Reference for the `sbc_mc` benchmark — the Monte Carlo of Shi, Xi & Xie (2025),
"A Synthetic Business Cycle Approach to Counterfactual Analysis with
Nonstationary Macroeconomic Data," arXiv:2505.22388, Section 4 / Table 1.
Authors' code: github.com/jinxi-atlas/Synthetic-business-cycle-code (no licence).

Files
- reference.R       Draws the panels from the authors' own DGP, ported from
                    their simulation/simulation_nonnegative.R -- the script
                    behind Table 1's non-negative-weights columns. Base R only:
                    the authors' estimation legs (Synth / kernlab ipop) are not
                    run here, so the capture reproduces on a bare R.
- panels.tsv.gz     60 replications of Models 1-3 at the Table 1 cell
                    T0 = 100, phi = 0.5, N + 1 = 12, h = p = Fh = 2, written at
                    %.17g. Model 1 is the authors' DGP 1.3 (mu ~ N(0, 1/4)).
- versions.txt      R version, source script, cell parameters, seed.

Why the split
- Generation is R's and estimation is mlsynth's. A Python port of the DGP would
  put the simulator and the estimator on the same side of the comparison, where
  a wrong port and a wrong estimator produce the same symptom. With the panels
  captured from the authors' code, a ratio that disagrees with Table 1 is the
  estimator's disagreement.
- The draw order inside a replication is the authors' own, including the two
  Model 1 designs whose panels are discarded (they advance the RNG stream) and
  Model 3's reuse of Model 2's `eps`.

What the case pins
- The paper's ordering claims, exactly: every post-treatment MSE ratio below 1,
  and Model 3's the highest (conventional SC is competitive under
  cointegration).
- The three ratios as values. The panels are read from disk and both estimators
  are deterministic, so a run reproduces them bit for bit. At 60 replications
  they are 0.0083 / 0.0222 / 0.7670 against Table 1's 10,000-replication
  0.01 / 0.09 / 0.47; the case reports that gap at the size it is instead of
  widening a band to cover it.

The authors' code is reproduced here for offline replication of this benchmark
only; all credit to the authors.
